// engine/level/bg.emp — Plane B (background) drawing routines (§2 A.5)
//
// T1 (zone-wide):     BG_Init loads act_bg_tiles into the shared BG VRAM region
//                     (slots 1024-1471, $8000-$B7FF — bounded above by the
//                     relocated SAT at $B800; see BG_TILE_REGION_BYTES below),
//                     then blits act_bg_layout into Plane B nametable. Both
//                     happen once at level load.
// PER-REGION:         Section_RedrawPlanes (in section.emp) blits the WINDOW of
//                     the REGION's layout that the live BG scroll selects, at
//                     level init and cache recovery only, with Act.act_bg_layout
//                     as the fallback when the region's rg_bg_layout is 0. It
//                     seeds BG_Plane_Top to that window (BG-PLANE-WINDOW,
//                     2026-09-16). Teleports no longer redraw.
//
// THE BACKGROUND BELONGS TO THE REGION, NOT TO THE SECTION (regions part 2,
// step 3, 2026-09-16; spec §4.5). `Sec.sec_bg_layout` is DELETED — Sec 26 -> 22
// bytes — and Section_RedrawPlanes now resolves through Region_Resolve on the
// camera centre, the same point and the same query Parallax_CheckBoundary's
// crossing and the init ladder's parallax boot select already use. Three private
// answers to "which background" would have been three chances to disagree; the
// 2026-08-26 precedence bug is what that looks like when it happens.
//
// Layout shape: each nametable layout is a raw 64x64 = 8192 bytes (full
// Plane B nametable — the BG is 512px tall for vertical parallax; the
// injector zero-pads 32-row layouts). Byte order is ROW-MAJOR:
// blob[(row*64 + col)*2] — each row's 64 columns are contiguous (row stride
// = 64 cols * 2 B = 128), which is BYTE-FOR-BYTE THE PLANE B NAMETABLE'S OWN
// VRAM ORDER. So a full-plane blit is one linear move.l run at autoinc $02
// (BG_Init below; Section_RedrawPlanes' Plane B half is the same run), and one
// plane ROW is a contiguous 64-word gather (Draw_BG_TileRow, engine/level/
// plane_buffer.emp). Both out-of-assembler writers now emit this order
// directly — tools/ojz_strip_gen.py always did, and tools/inject_editor_bg.py
// (which overwrites strip_gen's intermediate) stopped transposing.
//
// IT WAS COLUMN-MAJOR UNTIL REGIONS PART 2, STEP 2 (2026-09-15), and the flip
// is option R of that spec's §4.2. Column contiguity existed for one consumer,
// `Draw_BG_TileColumn`, which had ZERO callers for its whole life and is
// deleted in the same step; the column path returns in the big-levels parcel
// over a block map, not over this raw blob. What part 2 needs is the other
// axis: a background taller than the plane streams in by ROW, and the repaint
// at a region crossing sweeps row by row. Against a column-major blob a row
// producer is a 64-read strided gather at stride 128 — about twice the cost,
// on every streamed row, forever — while the two live blits had to be rewritten
// under part 2 regardless. HALF OF THAT HAS NOW HAPPENED: BG-PLANE-WINDOW
// (2026-09-16) made Section_RedrawPlanes blit a WINDOW of PLANE_V_CELLS rows
// rather than the head of the image — as two linear runs, not the row loop this
// comment predicted, precisely because row-major makes a run of consecutive map
// rows contiguous. Step 8's plane shrink still forces the same on BG_Init, which
// today is exempt because its source is exactly one plane.
//
// ALL 64 PLANE ROWS ARE LIVE (NEW-5 close, 2026-08-05). Both runtime
// maintainers of Plane B cover the full plane, and since BG-PLANE-WINDOW closed
// (2026-09-16) they do it differently: BG_Init drains the whole
// BG_LAYOUT_SIZE image (its source IS the plane, `ensure`d beside that constant),
// while Section_RedrawPlanes blits the PLANE_V_CELLS-row WINDOW the live BG
// scroll selects out of a region layout that may be taller — two runs, summing
// to PLANE_V_CELLS rows by construction. The old 32-row maintainer limit silently
// half-reverted any BG art above row 31 on a redraw; that was NOT
// hypothetical: the shipped OJZ override carries real art in rows 32-63, and
// the full-plane vscroll wrap (v_factor_bg with a 512px plane) puts those
// rows on screen, including at spawn. The 512px vertical headroom the shape
// advertises is therefore real, in every writer.
// Tile-blob shape: 2-byte big-endian length header + raw deduped tile bytes
//   (mirrors S4LZ blob shape so engine can read length without a separate field).
// Decision rationale: docs/research/per-section-background.md (Q4 + Q5).

module engine.bg in bg

use engine.constants.{VRAM_SPRITE_TABLE, VRAM_PLANE_B_BYTES, BG_TILE_BASE_VRAM, BG_TILE_CAPACITY, VDP_DATA, VDP_CTRL, PLANE_H_CELLS, PLANE_V_CELLS}
// Step 6's plane-buffer reservation `ensure` is stated against the buffer's own size.
use engine.constants.{PLANE_BUFFER_SIZE, DMA_BUDGET_NTSC}
use engine.constants.{SCREEN_HEIGHT}
use engine.structs.{Act, Region}
use engine.vdp.{VdpTarget, VdpOp, vdp_comm}
use engine.z80_bus.{z80_stopped}
// Regions part 2 step 5. The streamer reads the clamped map-space BG scroll that
// Parallax_Step5_Vscroll produced THIS frame, and its per-frame row budget is
// derived from that step's rate clamp rather than chosen. engine.parallax does
// not import this module, so the edge is acyclic.
use engine.parallax.{BG_VSCROLL_ROW_PX, BG_VSCROLL_MAX_STEP}
use engine.plane_buffer.{Draw_BG_TileRow}

// `pub` because it is a CONTRACT, not an internal: the act's BG blob must be
// exactly this long, and games/sonic4/data/levels/ojz/act1/act_assets.emp now
// types its embed with it so a wrong-sized blob is an `array length mismatch` at
// build time rather than a transposed background at runtime (tools lens sweep D6).
pub const BG_LAYOUT_SIZE  = 64*64*2     // 8192 bytes (full Plane B nametable)
// BG-PLANE-WINDOW (2026-09-16). This `ensure` is what lets `BG_Init` below keep a
// whole-image blit and a `clr.w BG_Plane_Top` while `Section_RedrawPlanes` grew a
// windowed one. The ACT DEFAULT layout is exactly one plane, so its only legal window is
// rows 0..PLANE_V_CELLS-1 and its only legal seed is 0 — and BG_Init cannot compute any
// other, because it runs from `Level_LoadArt`, which stands ABOVE `Camera_Init`, so no
// camera, no region and no scroll exist yet (BG-BOOT-REGION-BLIT). That made the seed
// correct-by-a-sentence; it is now correct-by-a-build-error. The REGION layouts
// (`Region.rg_bg_layout`) are NOT covered by this and are exactly the ones that can be
// taller — they are typed by their own size and windowed at the other blit.
ensure(BG_LAYOUT_SIZE == PLANE_H_CELLS * PLANE_V_CELLS * 2,
       "engine/level/bg.emp: BG_LAYOUT_SIZE is {BG_LAYOUT_SIZE} bytes but the Plane B nametable is PLANE_H_CELLS x PLANE_V_CELLS x 2 = {PLANE_H_CELLS * PLANE_V_CELLS * 2}. The act default layout is the ONE background image no writer windows: BG_Init drains it whole and seeds BG_Plane_Top to the constant 0, which is correct only while the image IS the plane. If the act default may now be a different height, BG_Init needs the windowed prime Section_RedrawPlanes has — and it cannot have one, because it runs before Camera_Init and no scroll exists at that site. Either restore the equality or move the act-default blit below the camera")
// The shared BG tile region runs from BG_TILE_BASE_VRAM up to the relocated
// sprite attr table at $B800 — so $8000..$B7FF = 448 tiles is the PHYSICAL run.
// It is no longer the clamp: the top of that run has been carved away by other
// regions, so the BG arena owns only BG_TILE_CAPACITY of it and a full 448-tile
// blob would spray over its neighbours' art rather than over the SAT. The clamp
// below uses the declared capacity; this constant stays because the `ensure`
// under it is what keeps the two facts from crossing.
//
// NO FIGURE IS STATED HERE ON PURPOSE. This comment twice said "400" and twice
// named a slot range, and both went stale under carves it never saw: the arena
// went 448 -> 400 (waterline strips) -> 388 -> 376 (the spring, in two edits),
// while the prose sat still. A fresh literal would stale on the identical clock.
// The live values are `BG_TILE_CAPACITY` itself (asserted against vram.toml by
// the game's own `ensure`) and the generated map, `docs/generated/vram-map-<game>.md`
// — read one of those, never a sentence.
const BG_TILE_REGION_BYTES = VRAM_SPRITE_TABLE - BG_TILE_BASE_VRAM   // $3800 = 14336 B = 448 tiles
const BG_TILE_CAPACITY_BYTES = BG_TILE_CAPACITY * 32                 // the arena's declared size in bytes; see above for why no total is written here
ensure(BG_TILE_CAPACITY_BYTES <= BG_TILE_REGION_BYTES,
       "games/<game>/vram.toml declares bg_region larger than the physical $8000..$B7FF run: BG_TILE_CAPACITY = {BG_TILE_CAPACITY} tiles = {BG_TILE_CAPACITY_BYTES} bytes against {BG_TILE_REGION_BYTES}. The BG arena is bounded above by the relocated sprite attribute table at VRAM_SPRITE_TABLE, so a capacity past it is not a bigger arena, it is a blob clamp that lets act art overwrite the SAT")

// -----------------------------------------------
// BG_Init — load shared BG tiles + blit Plane B nametable.
//
// In:  a0 = Act descriptor pointer (read only — preserved)
// Out: none
// Clobbers: d0, d1, a1, a2
//
// Display assumed off (called from Level_LoadArt, before display enable).
// Both writes are blocking via VDP DATA port; autoincrement = 2 bytes/word.
// The Z80 bus is held for the full copy brackets (~21ms + ~12ms worst case)
// — music already playing through level load takes an audible DAC gap
// (accepted at load; the VBlank flag-bracket idiom is the alternative if
// load-time audio continuity ever matters).
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc BG_Init (a0: *Act) clobbers(d0-d1/a1-a2) preserves(a3/sr) {
        movem.l a3, -(sp)
        movea.l a0, a3                  // a3 = act ptr (preserve)
        // Mask interrupts for the blocking VDP copies: a VBlank mid-copy lets
        // the VInt handler repoint the VDP address and the remainder of the
        // blob sprays to wrong addresses (large BG blobs span >1 frame of
        // copy loop). Mirrors Section_RedrawPlanes' guard.
        move.w  sr, -(sp)
        move.w  #$2700, sr

        // --- load BG tile blob into shared region at BG_TILE_BASE_VRAM ---
        // THE TILE TRACKER SEED (region bg switch, 2026-09-16). What the arena will hold is
        // the act default, and no overwrite is in flight. Seeded BEFORE the copy for the same
        // reason BG_Plane_Layout is seeded before its blit (a1 is consumed by the copy), and
        // unconditionally, so a null act blob records "holds nothing" rather than whatever the
        // previous act left. The band hold is clear: the arena holds the act's own tiles, the
        // ones its BgAnim bands animate.
        movea.l Act.act_bg_tiles(a3), a1
        move.l  a1, BG_Tiles_Current            // (BG_Tiles_Current).w
        clr.l   BG_Tiles_Target                 // (BG_Tiles_Target).w
        clr.w   BG_Tiles_Offset                 // (BG_Tiles_Offset).w
        clr.b   BG_Bands_Hold                   // (BG_Bands_Hold).w
        cmpa.w  #0, a1
        beq     .skip_tiles
        with z80_stopped {
            jbsr    BG_UploadTiles              // blocking copy, guards inside; a1 consumed
        }
    .skip_tiles:

        // --- blit BG nametable to Plane B ---
        movea.l Act.act_bg_layout(a3), a1
        cmpa.w  #0, a1
        beq     .skip_nt

        // THE TRACKER SEED, AND WHY IT NAMES THE ACT DEFAULT AND NOT A REGION.
        // The step-3 spec (§4.5, boot half) asks this routine to resolve the boot
        // region first and blit THAT row's layout, on the stated premise that
        // "Camera_Init precedes the boot select". That premise is true of the
        // PARALLAX boot select and false of this routine, MEASURED at the one
        // shipped ladder (games/sonic4/test/ojz_scroll_test.emp): `jbsr
        // Level_LoadArt` — which tail-calls this proc from engine/level/load_art.emp
        // — stands ~48 lines ABOVE `jbsr Camera_Init`, and Camera_Init is the only
        // writer of Camera_X/Y before the plane fill. A Region_Resolve here would
        // therefore query the camera centre of a camera that does not exist yet:
        // (0,0) + half a screen after boot's RAM clear, or the PREVIOUS act's
        // position on a re-entry. It would answer, it would answer wrongly, and
        // today it would answer wrongly in a way no picture could show, because
        // all ten of act 1's rows default their layout. That is the shape this
        // parcel's step-2 sibling refused: an instrument that cannot produce the
        // answer it claims to look for.
        //
        // Nothing is lost by declining. The display is still OFF here (this runs
        // from Level_LoadArt, before display enable), and the region-aware blit —
        // Section_RedrawPlanes, reached through Section_Plane_Dirty — runs later in
        // the SAME init, after Camera_Init and after the DEBUG boot-position
        // override, and re-seeds all four cells. So the first VISIBLE frame is the
        // region's picture on every path, and the invariant the later steps rest on
        // ("the tracker names the blob the plane holds") is true at every instant in
        // between rather than from the second writer onwards.
        //
        // Booked in docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md as BG-BOOT-REGION-BLIT, which is also
        // where the ordering fix belongs if a future act ever needs the FIRST blit
        // to be the right one.
        // THE SEED IS THE CONSTANT 0 HERE, AND IT IS PROVED RATHER THAN ASSUMED
        // (BG-PLANE-WINDOW, closed 2026-09-16). Section_RedrawPlanes' Plane B half now
        // primes the WINDOW the scroll selects; this one does not, because the image it
        // blits is the ACT DEFAULT, typed `[u8; BG_LAYOUT_SIZE]`, and the `ensure` beside
        // BG_LAYOUT_SIZE above pins that to exactly one plane. Rows 0..PLANE_V_CELLS-1 are
        // therefore the whole map and 0 is the only window there is. It could not compute a
        // different one in any case: the ordering fact recorded below (Level_LoadArt stands
        // above Camera_Init) means no camera, no region and no scroll exist at this site.
        move.l  a1, BG_Plane_Layout             // (BG_Plane_Layout).w — what the plane will hold
        clr.w   BG_Plane_Top                    // (BG_Plane_Top).w — window top = map row 0
        clr.w   BG_Wipe_Cursor                  // (BG_Wipe_Cursor).w — cursor + resume row, one word
        // Second consumer of the $2700 mask above. The mask is STILL load-bearing
        // after the row-major flip, and for the reason that outlived the
        // autoincrement excursion: the VInt handler repoints the VDP address
        // register, so a VBlank landing inside this 8192-byte blocking copy sends
        // the remainder of the image to whatever address the handler left behind.
        // (The excursion itself is gone — the blit now runs at the default autoinc
        // $02 from first word to last, so Flush_VDP_Shadow's every-VBlank reg $0F
        // re-blit can no longer contradict it. That removes one of the two hazards
        // the mask covers, not both.)
        if DEBUG == 1 {
            move.w  sr, d0
            andi.w  #$0700, d0
            assert.w d0, hs, #$0600     // IPL >= 6: no VBlank can land mid-copy
        }
        with z80_stopped {
            // ROW-MAJOR blit, and it is a straight linear copy: the layout blob's
            // byte order (blob[(row*64 + col)*2]) IS the Plane B nametable's VRAM
            // order, so the whole BG_LAYOUT_SIZE image drains as one autoinc-$02
            // move.l run with a single VDP command setup. The 64 per-column command
            // setups the column-major blit needed are gone with it.
            //
            // No length guard needed: the geometry is the compile-time constant
            // BG_LAYOUT_SIZE, never derived from blob data, so the sub-longword
            // underflow that bites the tile blit above cannot occur — the layout
            // blob is a fixed 8192 B image by contract (act_assets.emp types the
            // embed with BG_LAYOUT_SIZE, so a wrong-sized blob is a build error).
            //
            // The count is BG_LAYOUT_SIZE/4 - 1 = 2047, which is past a moveq's
            // signed byte, so it loads with a move.w. Do not "optimise" it back.
            move.w  #$8F02, VDP_CTRL       // (VDP_CTRL).l — autoincrement 2 (one cell)
            move.l  #vdp_comm(VRAM_PLANE_B_BYTES, VdpTarget.Vram, VdpOp.Write), VDP_CTRL
            lea     VDP_DATA, a2           // (VDP_DATA).l
            move.w  #BG_LAYOUT_SIZE/4 - 1, d0
        .nt_copy:
            move.l  (a1)+, (a2)
            dbf     d0, .nt_copy
        }
    .skip_nt:
        move.w  (sp)+, sr               // restore interrupt mask
        movem.l (sp)+, a3
        rts
}

// BG_UploadTiles sits AFTER BG_Init on purpose: BG_Init is the `bg` section's head label,
// and sigil's packing walk keys the section's declared alignment on that label
// (`sigil_harness::section_align::DECLARED`). A new proc first in the file would rename the
// head and fail the build with [layout.undeclared-alignment] (measured 2026-09-16).
// -----------------------------------------------
// BG_UploadTiles — blocking copy of one BG tile blob into the BG arena at BG_TILE_BASE_VRAM.
//
// In:  a1 = tile blob (BE u16 byte length + raw 4bpp tiles), nonzero
// Out: none
// Clobbers: d1, a1-a2
//
// THE CALLER OWNS THE POSTURE: interrupts masked (a VBlank mid-copy repoints the VDP address
// and the rest of the blob sprays elsewhere) and the Z80 kept off the bus (BG_Init holds it
// with `z80_stopped`; Section_RedrawPlanes' storm uses the SND_CTRL_DMA_ACTIVE flag bracket
// under sound, or holds the bus without it). It does not record what it copied: the caller
// writes BG_Tiles_Current, next to the decision that chose the blob.
//
// Factored out of BG_Init on 2026-09-16 (region bg switch, task 2) so the synchronous region
// path copies through the SAME guards rather than a second copy of them. The guard commentary
// below moved with the code.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc BG_UploadTiles (a1: *u8) clobbers(d1/a1-a2) {
        if DEBUG == 1 {
            move.w  sr, d1
            andi.w  #$0700, d1
            assert.w d1, hs, #$0600     // IPL >= 6: no VBlank can land mid-copy
        }
        move.w  (a1)+, d1               // d1.w = tile-bytes length (uncompressed)
        beq     .done
        // Capacity guard (last line of defense): clamp the copy length to what the
        // BG arena is DECLARED to own — BG_TILE_CAPACITY tiles, not the physical
        // $8000..$B7FF run. Above the arena sit `waterline_strips` (48 tiles, item
        // 9d) and then the SAT at $B800, and a blob longer than the declared
        // capacity would overwrite the first before it ever reached the second.
        // The build tools should keep BG art within budget (inject_editor_bg.py
        // gates the final blob on the same constant); this is what guarantees no
        // corruption of either neighbour if a generator gate drifts.
        cmpi.w  #BG_TILE_CAPACITY_BYTES, d1
        bls     .bg_len_ok
        move.w  #BG_TILE_CAPACITY_BYTES, d1
    .bg_len_ok:
        // Tier-1 blit: copy the tile body a LONGWORD at a time (autoinc 2 keeps
        // it linear in VRAM). The count is quartered, so the length guard must be
        // RE-DERIVED — the parcel-6 word-count guard (lsr #1) does not survive the
        // move.l translation, it just underflows on a different set of lengths.
        //
        // The clamp above bounds the copy from ABOVE. It does NOT bound it from
        // below: a drifted length of 1..3 bytes passes the `beq` (nonzero) and the
        // `bls` (in range), then lsr.w #2 -> 0 and subq -> $FFFF, and dbf would run
        // 65,536 LONGWORDS = 256KB straight through the SAT and every other VRAM
        // structure. Close it by taking the LONGWORD count FIRST and skipping on
        // zero: lengths 1,2,3 all lsr#2 -> 0 -> `beq` skips BEFORE the subq, so the
        // underflow is unreachable. Lengths >= 4 copy floor(len/4) longwords and
        // drop the sub-longword tail (len 5 -> 1 longword + 1 dropped byte). Real
        // blobs are 32-byte granular, and BOTH producers now assert len%4==0 —
        // ojz_strip_gen.py (the unconditional emitter; assert added NEW-4
        // 2026-08-05) and inject_editor_bg.py, and tools/gen_region_bg_showcase.py
        // refuses a non-32-byte-granular body — so the drop never fires in practice;
        // it is the last line of defense.
        //
        // (The note that used to sit here about hoisting the counter ops above the
        // `z80_stopped` bracket is moot inside this proc: the bracket is the caller's,
        // so an early `rts` cannot leave the Z80 halted.)
        lsr.w   #2, d1                  // longword count = bytes/4
        beq     .done                   // 1..3-byte (sub-long) blob -> nothing to copy
        subq.w  #1, d1                  // dbf counter (safe: longword count >= 1)
        move.w  #$8F02, VDP_CTRL        // (VDP_CTRL).l — autoincrement = 2
        move.l  #vdp_comm(BG_TILE_BASE_VRAM, VdpTarget.Vram, VdpOp.Write), VDP_CTRL
        lea     VDP_DATA, a2            // (VDP_DATA).l
    .tile_copy:
        move.l  (a1)+, (a2)
        dbf     d1, .tile_copy
    .done:
        rts
}


// ===========================================================================
// THE STEADY-STATE ROW STREAMER (regions part 2, step 5; spec §4.2, "Steady-state
// streaming"). Plane B stops being the whole background picture and becomes a
// 64-row WINDOW onto a taller map, exactly the way Plane A is already "a 64-cell
// ring the world wraps through" (engine/level/section.emp).
//
// THE RING IS FREE, AND THAT IS THE ONE FACT TO READ FIRST. The VDP maps screen
// line L to plane line (L + vscroll) mod PLANE_B_SPAN, so the plane row showing
// map row m is ALWAYS m mod PLANE_V_CELLS — no extra arithmetic, no rebasing, no
// mapping table. The whole of streaming is therefore one question: which 64
// consecutive MAP rows does the plane currently hold? That number is
// `BG_Plane_Top` and this proc is its only mover after the two synchronous blits
// seed it.
//
// ---- THE WINDOW RULE, AND ITS CONTAINMENT PROOF ----
//
//   want_top = clamp( (vscroll >> 3) - BG_STREAM_LEAD_ROWS,  0,  map_rows - PLANE_V_CELLS )
//
// It is ANCHORED ON THE SCROLL, not hysteretic ("move only when a visible row
// falls off"). Both keep the picture correct; the anchored form was chosen for
// three reasons and the third is the one that matters:
//   1. No direction state and no edge cases — one formula, both ways.
//   2. The lead is centred, so there is slack in BOTH directions. A hysteretic
//      tracker has ZERO slack in the direction of travel by construction: the row
//      that must be drawn is the row that just became visible, so a single
//      refused entry (plane buffer full that frame) is a garbage row ON SCREEN.
//      With a centred lead a refusal is invisible and self-heals next frame.
//   3. It absorbs a region crossing. Cross into a row whose map is taller and
//      `max_top` jumps 0 -> 32 in one frame, so `want_top` can jump by up to the
//      lead at once. The containment proof below holds at every intermediate
//      `BG_Plane_Top` on the way, so the catch-up is invisible rather than being
//      a tear that step 6's wipe has to hide.
//
// CONTAINMENT, derived (this is what the three `ensure`s below pin). The screen
// shows map rows [vrow, vbot] where vrow = vscroll >> 3 and vbot <= vrow +
// BG_SCREEN_ROWS - 1. The plane holds [top, top + PLANE_V_CELLS - 1]. Need
// top <= vrow and top + PLANE_V_CELLS - 1 >= vbot, i.e.
//     0 <= BG_STREAM_LEAD_ROWS <= PLANE_V_CELLS - BG_SCREEN_ROWS.
// At the low clamp (top = 0): vrow <= LEAD, so vbot <= LEAD + ROWS-1 <= 63. At the
// high clamp (top = map_rows - PLANE_V_CELLS): the largest legal vscroll is
// span - SCREEN_HEIGHT (Parallax_Step5_Vscroll's position clamp), whose vbot is
// exactly map_rows - 1 = top + PLANE_V_CELLS - 1. The two clamps meet the two
// bounds EXACTLY, which is why the position clamp of step 4 had to land first.
//
// ---- WHY IT CANNOT FALL BEHIND, WHICH IS STEP 4's RATE CLAMP DOING THIS JOB ----
// `want_top` is a monotone function of `vscroll` with slope 1/8, and step 4 bounds
// |dvscroll| by BG_VSCROLL_MAX_STEP per frame. So |dwant_top| <= BG_VSCROLL_MAX_STEP
// / BG_VSCROLL_ROW_PX = BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS rows per frame, which is exactly this
// proc's per-frame budget. The tracker therefore NEVER accumulates debt: it is not
// "fast enough in practice", it is bounded by construction, and the `ensure` below
// is what keeps the two constants tied together if either moves.
//
// ---- THE SWEEP ORDER, AND THE ONE DESIGN CALL OF STEP 6 ----
// The sweep starts at the TOP VISIBLE plane row and walks DOWN, wrapping, covering all
// PLANE_V_CELLS plane rows exactly once. It is FIXED: no entry axis, no direction byte.
//
// §4.3 asks instead for a sweep "from the entry side", starting past the far edge of the
// screen "so hidden rows repaint first". Three things are wrong with that here, and each
// is measurable rather than a matter of taste:
//
//   1. A HORIZONTAL ENTRY CARRIES NO ROW ORDER. A row spans the whole plane width, so
//      crossing left-to-right changes nothing about which rows the screen shows. There is
//      no "far edge of the screen" along the axis a row sweep can walk.
//   2. A FALL HAS NO HORIZONTAL COMPONENT AT ALL. Measured before this step and booked as
//      REGIONS-VERTICAL-CROSSING-ON-LANDING: leaving DEBUG free flight at (5700, 1000)
//      drops the camera centre to y 3034 and resolves a different region, with the camera
//      X unmoved. An entry-side rule has no value to read there.
//   3. HIDDEN-FIRST MAXIMISES THE ONE TRANSIENT E2 COULD NOT JUDGE. The E2 verdict
//      (2026-09-16, confirming the owner's own 2026-09-14 ruling) is that the palette
//      SNAPS on frame 0 and the rows sweep after it. So from frame 0 the visible rows are
//      showing the NEW palette over the OLD art, and that is precisely the state E2 said
//      it could not evaluate because it did not exist yet. Repainting the ~BG_SCREEN_ROWS
//      hidden rows first would hold that state for the whole sweep; repainting the visible
//      ones first clears it in ceil(BG_SCREEN_ROWS / BG_WIPE_ROWS_PER_FRAME) frames and
//      leaves the rest to run out of sight.
//
// ---- WHAT S3K ACTUALLY DOES, RE-VERIFIED FIRSTHAND, AND WHERE IT DISAGREES WITH THIS ----
// (2026-09-16 sweep over ten trees. Two claims that reached this parcel as received wisdom
// were WRONG at the source and are corrected here rather than repeated.)
//
// S3K's Plane B is 64 x 32 cells = 512 x 256 px (`VRAM_Plane_B_Name_Table = $E000 ; Extends
// until $EFFF`, sonic3k.constants.asm:1064), so it is SIXTEEN block-rows tall, not 64. Its
// background repaints are a 4-state machine per zone (SSZ1: sonic3k.asm:116430-116561) that
// seeds `Draw_delayed_rowcount = $F` and walks `Draw_delayed_position` by $10 a row through
// the ORDINARY row producer (`jsr Setup_TileRowDraw`, :103450) into the same deferred plane
// buffer the streamer uses, alongside the streamer in the same frame. All of that matches
// what aeon does here. TWO THINGS DO NOT:
//
//   * `rowcount = $F` IS THE WHOLE PLANE HEIGHT. The camera-span test at :103444-103448 is
//     not a budget mechanism and does not make the sweep a subset: the plane is 256 px and
//     the test's window is 240, so it drops about ONE row, plus whatever the camera scrolls
//     past mid-sweep. It is a live-camera guard. (The claim that S3K "skips rows outside the
//     visible span RATHER THAN drawing all 64" is false twice over — there are no 64 rows,
//     and it does draw them all.) So S3K is NOT an economy this file declines; it repaints
//     its whole plane, exactly as this does.
//   * THE RATE IS NOT FLAT. SSZ1's first pass uses the 2-row wrapper
//     (`Draw_PlaneVertBottomUp`, :103427) and its second the 1-row one, and the cap is the
//     `$480`-byte `Plane_buffer` (sonic3k.constants.asm:514) — about four block-rows — not
//     CPU time. Aeon's BG_WIPE_ROWS_PER_FRAME is capped by the same resource for the same
//     reason; see the reservation `ensure` above.
//
// ---- THE COUNTER-PRECEDENT, WHICH IS REAL AND IS ANSWERED RATHER THAN OMITTED ----
// S3K's house order is BOTTOM-UP and FIXED (47 bottom-up call sites against 6 top-down),
// seeded at `camY+$E0` regardless of the crossing's sign — SSZ1, ICZ1, ICZ2, AIZ1, CNZ1 all
// do that, and every one of them triggers on a camera Y crossing with no horizontal
// component, so the vertical case §4.3 had no answer for is S3K's NORMAL case.
//
// FLYING BATTERY IS THE EXCEPTION AND IT DOES TIE THE ORDER TO TRAVEL, on all four axes:
// `FBZ1_BackgroundEvent_Index` (sonic3k.asm:108708-108718) has TopDown / BottomUp /
// LeftRight / RightLeft entry points and `FBZ1_CheckBGChange` picks one per crossing. Seven
// independent decision sites agree on the rule (:108978-108988, :109020-109031,
// :109062-109073, :109110-109122, :109155-109168, :109182-109191, and FBZ2 at
// :109784-109796): SEED AT THE EDGE YOU ARE TRAVELLING TOWARD AND WALK AWAY FROM IT.
// Descending seeds the BOTTOM row and steps up. That is the opposite of §4.3's
// "far edge, hidden rows first" — and on a descent it is also the opposite of the seed
// chosen here.
//
// IT IS NOT ADOPTED, for two reasons that are about this plane rather than about taste:
//
//   1. ITS HORIZONTAL HALF NEEDS A COLUMN PRODUCER AND WE DELETED OURS. FBZ answers a
//      horizontal crossing with a COLUMN sweep. Aeon's sweep is rows only (the column path
//      returns in the big-levels parcel, over a block map). Adopting FBZ's vertical half
//      alone would leave the horizontal case with no principled seed and reintroduce the
//      direction byte for a rule that covers one of the two axes.
//   2. WE HAVE A LEAD AND FBZ DOES NOT, and that changes which tail is worth having. FBZ's
//      plane is 16 block-rows against a 15-row screen: essentially all of it is visible and
//      there is no hidden tail to order. Aeon's window is 64 rows against
//      BG_SCREEN_ROWS visible, with BG_STREAM_LEAD_ROWS hidden above and the rest hidden
//      below, none of which the steady-state tracker ever revisits. On a DESCENT — the case
//      that arrives without the player walking anywhere — the hidden rows BELOW are the ones
//      about to be revealed and the hidden rows ABOVE are the ones leaving for good.
//      "Top visible row, walking down, wrapping" orders the whole plane as
//      visible -> about-to-be-revealed -> leaving, which is the right order for a descent
//      and for a stationary window, and is merely late on an ascent's hidden rows (which the
//      streamer is drawing at the top edge anyway). FBZ's seed has no such tail to get right.
//
// WHERE THE TWO AGREE, which is the part that matters: both repaint what the player is
// looking at before what they are not. The disagreement is confined to the ORDER WITHIN the
// visible span on a vertical entry, which lasts
// ceil(BG_SCREEN_ROWS / BG_WIPE_ROWS_PER_FRAME) ticks — measured at 8, over which the screen
// drifted 6 rows of 29, so every row visible at the arm was still visible at the end.
//
// NOBODY PAINTS OFF-SCREEN FIRST. Across all ten trees swept, not one does: S3K and S.C.E.
// actively skip off-screen rows, Alien Soldier and Batman & Robin paint on-screen first by
// construction. §4.3's hidden-rows-first sweep has no precedent in the corpus.
//
// AND ONE PRECEDENT FOR A PALETTE HOLD EXISTS, named because E2's row 3 stays cheap on
// purpose: ICZ1 holds the old palette through TWO full sweeps and swaps on the frame the
// second completes (sonic3k.asm:110249-110253, `ICZ1_SetIndoorPal`), about 16 frames. FBZ
// and ICZ2 do the opposite — swap first, wipe after — and mitigate by changing only 8 of 16
// colours. Alien Soldier offers a third: run the sweep with the display ON and mask it with
// a concurrent palette FADE-IN. Aeon follows FBZ/ICZ2 (snap first) per the E2 verdict; the
// other two are what row 3 would buy if the transient reads badly on a real fall.
//
// WHY A PLANE ROW IS STORED AND NOT A MAP ROW. During a fall the window is moving while
// the sweep runs. A plane row is a physical nametable address and the map row it shows is
// a function of BG_Plane_Top at the instant of the draw, so deriving the map row keeps
// every draw correct for what that row is currently displaying. Storing a map row (or an
// offset from the window top) does not: with the window advancing one row a frame and the
// cursor advancing BG_WIPE_ROWS_PER_FRAME, the covered PLANE rows would step by more than
// one and the sweep would leave holes. There is no hole in the plane-row form, only the
// possibility of a row being drawn twice — once by the sweep and once by the streamer when
// it enters the window — and both draw it from the same new blob.
//
// ---- WHAT THIS PROC DOES NOT DO, named so a green is not misread ----
// Its steady-state half is the steady state only. A PRIME — the plane holding the wrong window
// outright — is not reachable from here at 2 rows a frame, and the one path that
// can produce one is `Section_RedrawPlanes` (cache recovery, and the DEBUG warp).
// THAT PATH NOW PRIMES THE WINDOW RATHER THAN ROWS 0..63 (BG-PLANE-WINDOW, closed
// 2026-09-16): it restates the `want_top` rule above against
// `Parallax_Current_Vscroll_BG`, seeds `BG_Plane_Top` to it and blits the window as
// two runs, so this proc finds the tracker already where the scroll wants it and
// moves nothing. The number it needs is live on both ladders because
// `Parallax_Init` now stands ABOVE the boot `st Section_Plane_Dirty` pair; the warp
// ladder needed no change, since the rate clamp deliberately holds the scroll near
// its pre-warp value across a teleport. WHAT IS STILL OPEN AND IS NOT THIS: the
// scroll itself ratchets to its post-warp target at BG_VSCROLL_MAX_STEP a frame with
// no prime exemption (BG-RATE-PRIME-EXEMPTION), and the parallax BANDS alias against
// plane space on a taller map (BG-BAND-PLANE-ANCHOR). Neither is the nametable.
// ===========================================================================

// One nametable row in pixels. Taken from parallax rather than re-spelled: the
// scroll this proc reads is stated in the same units, and BG_VSCROLL_ROW_PX
// already carries the ensure tying it to the plane's own cell height.
pub const BG_STREAM_ROW_PX   = BG_VSCROLL_ROW_PX
// Map rows the screen can touch at once: 224/8 = 28 whole rows, plus one because
// a window origin is not row-aligned, so 29 rows are partially visible at worst.
pub const BG_SCREEN_ROWS     = SCREEN_HEIGHT / BG_STREAM_ROW_PX + 1
// Rows the plane holds that the screen cannot show — the total slack available.
pub const BG_STREAM_SPARE_ROWS = PLANE_V_CELLS - BG_SCREEN_ROWS
// Half of it above the screen, the rest below. CENTRED, not maximal on either
// side: see reason 2 and reason 3 in the header.
pub const BG_STREAM_LEAD_ROWS  = BG_STREAM_SPARE_ROWS / 2
// The per-frame row budget, DERIVED from step 4's rate clamp and not tuned.
pub const BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS   = BG_VSCROLL_MAX_STEP / BG_STREAM_ROW_PX

// ---- THE CROSSING WIPE's budget (regions part 2, step 6; spec §4.3) ----
// How many rows the sweep may spend in one frame. E1 (2026-09-16) measured the plane
// buffer's headroom and picked decision row 1 = 4; the arithmetic is RE-DERIVED below
// rather than carried, because E1's own total was 536 + 528 = 1064 and it OMITTED the
// steady-state streamer's share — correctly, at the time, since `Draw_BG_TileColumn` had
// zero callers and `BG_Stream_Update` did not exist until step 5. It exists now and it
// runs in the SAME frame as the wipe, so the real worst case is 536 + 264 + 528 = 1328.
pub const BG_WIPE_ROWS_PER_FRAME = 4
// The sweep covers every PLANE row exactly once — not every MAP row. See BG_Stream_Update's
// wipe arm for why that is the invariant that survives the window moving mid-wipe.
pub const BG_WIPE_TOTAL_ROWS     = PLANE_V_CELLS
// Frames a complete sweep takes with no refusals. The gate derives its bound from this.
pub const BG_WIPE_FRAMES         = (BG_WIPE_TOTAL_ROWS + BG_WIPE_ROWS_PER_FRAME - 1) / BG_WIPE_ROWS_PER_FRAME

// ---- THE PLANE-BUFFER RESERVATION, derived from the producers' OWN reservations ----
// Each number below is the byte figure the named routine adds to `Plane_Buffer_Ptr` before
// its own overflow test, restated here so a producer that changes its entry shape and this
// budget cannot drift apart silently.
const BG_ROW_ENTRY_BYTES = 4 + PLANE_H_CELLS*2      // Draw_BG_TileRow      (engine/level/plane_buffer.emp)
const FG_COL_ENTRY_BYTES = 8 + PLANE_V_CELLS*2      // Draw_TileColumn      (ditto)
const FG_ROW_ENTRY_BYTES = 4 + PLANE_H_CELLS*2      // Draw_TileRow_FromCache (ditto)
// The foreground's worst frame: the camera is capped at 16 px on each axis over an 8 px
// cell, so at most two columns and two rows enter per frame (E1's derivation, re-spelled
// from the two camera caps rather than quoted as "536").
const FG_PEAK_BYTES      = 2 * FG_COL_ENTRY_BYTES + 2 * FG_ROW_ENTRY_BYTES
const BG_PEAK_BYTES      = (BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS + BG_WIPE_ROWS_PER_FRAME) * BG_ROW_ENTRY_BYTES
ensure(FG_PEAK_BYTES + BG_PEAK_BYTES <= PLANE_BUFFER_SIZE - 2,
       "engine/level/bg.emp: one frame can now ask the plane buffer for {FG_PEAK_BYTES} B of foreground (2 columns at {FG_COL_ENTRY_BYTES} + 2 rows at {FG_ROW_ENTRY_BYTES}) plus {BG_PEAK_BYTES} B of background ({BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS} streamed + {BG_WIPE_ROWS_PER_FRAME} wiped rows at {BG_ROW_ENTRY_BYTES}), which is more than PLANE_BUFFER_SIZE - 2 = {PLANE_BUFFER_SIZE - 2}. NOTHING CRASHES IF THIS IS WRONG, which is why it is a build error: Draw_BG_TileRow REFUSES past the ceiling and the wipe cursor does not advance, so an over-budget frame shows up only as a wipe that takes longer than BG_WIPE_FRAMES ({BG_WIPE_FRAMES}) — and Draw_TileColumn's release arm drops a FOREGROUND column silently, which is a strip of level that is never redrawn. Lower BG_WIPE_ROWS_PER_FRAME (the wipe just takes more frames) or raise PLANE_BUFFER_SIZE")
ensure(BG_WIPE_ROWS_PER_FRAME >= 1 && BG_WIPE_ROWS_PER_FRAME <= BG_WIPE_TOTAL_ROWS,
       "engine/level/bg.emp: BG_WIPE_ROWS_PER_FRAME is {BG_WIPE_ROWS_PER_FRAME}, outside 1 .. {BG_WIPE_TOTAL_ROWS}. At 0 an armed wipe never retires its cursor and the plane holds a mix of two backgrounds forever; above the plane's own row count the `subq.b`/`beq` pair below would run the cursor past 0 and wrap it to 255")
ensure(BG_WIPE_TOTAL_ROWS <= 255,
       "engine/level/bg.emp: BG_Wipe_Cursor (engine/ram.emp) is a BYTE and BG_WIPE_TOTAL_ROWS is {BG_WIPE_TOTAL_ROWS}. `move.b #BG_WIPE_TOTAL_ROWS, BG_Wipe_Cursor` would truncate and the sweep would leave part of the plane holding the old background")

ensure(BG_STREAM_SPARE_ROWS > 0,
       "engine/level/bg.emp: the Plane B ring holds {PLANE_V_CELLS} rows and the screen can touch {BG_SCREEN_ROWS} of them at once, so there is no slack at all (BG_STREAM_SPARE_ROWS = {BG_STREAM_SPARE_ROWS}). BG_Stream_Update's containment proof needs at least one spare row; at zero or below the plane cannot hold the visible window plus any lead and every streamed row would be one the screen is already showing")
ensure(BG_STREAM_LEAD_ROWS >= 0 && BG_STREAM_LEAD_ROWS <= BG_STREAM_SPARE_ROWS,
       "engine/level/bg.emp: BG_STREAM_LEAD_ROWS is {BG_STREAM_LEAD_ROWS}, outside [0, {BG_STREAM_SPARE_ROWS}]. That range IS the containment proof in BG_Stream_Update's header: below 0 the window starts under the screen top and the topmost visible rows hold the wrong map row; above the spare the window ends above the screen bottom and the bottom rows do. Neither shows as a build error and both look like a torn background")
ensure(BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS * BG_STREAM_ROW_PX >= BG_VSCROLL_MAX_STEP,
       "engine/level/bg.emp: BG_Stream_Update can move the window {BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS} row(s) = {BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS * BG_STREAM_ROW_PX} px a frame, but engine/level/parallax.emp's rate clamp lets the BG scroll move {BG_VSCROLL_MAX_STEP} px a frame. The tracker would fall further behind on every frame of a sustained vertical run and NEVER catch up — the window lags, the lead is eaten, and the background tears at the trailing edge with nothing in the build to say so. Either raise BG_VSCROLL_MAX_STEP_ROWS' partner here or lower the rate clamp")
// ---- THE TILE OVERWRITE'S CHUNK (region bg switch, task 3; plan call C3 as amended) ----
// How many arena bytes one overwrite DMA carries. ONE chunk is outstanding at a time, so this is
// also the per-frame upload rate. The value is DERIVED against the NTSC window's worst frame
// and the derivation needs the GAME's own DMA riders (its DPLC peaks), which this engine file
// cannot see: tools/test_bg_overwrite_chunk_budget.py computes it from source and fails the
// build's pytest lane if this number no longer fits, or is not the largest 32-byte multiple
// that does. The engine-side half is the `ensure` below.
//
// The window, as that test derives it: DMA_BUDGET_NTSC, minus the plane drain (the FOREGROUND
// peak only, FG_PEAK_BYTES: the BG streamer and wipe are suspended for the whole overwrite, so
// their rows cannot share a chunk's frame), minus the Critical queue's peak (4 palette lines,
// the SAT, the HScroll table, and a full-CRAM raster ship), minus the Important queue's player
// DPLC peak (Sonic + Tails + tail, the duo cast dplc_straddle reserves for), minus every other
// Deferrable producer's peak that can sit AHEAD of the chunk in the FIFO (insta-shield and
// spindash-dust DPLC, waterline art). Act art page landings are NOT charged: they are the
// priority C1 chose, so a heavy streaming frame just delays the chunk.
pub const BG_OVERWRITE_CHUNK_BYTES = 256
const BG_CRITICAL_PEAK_BYTES = 4 * 32 + 640 + 896 + 128   // pinned to BuildStaticDMA by the test above
ensure(BG_OVERWRITE_CHUNK_BYTES >= 32 && BG_OVERWRITE_CHUNK_BYTES % 32 == 0,
       "engine/level/bg.emp: BG_OVERWRITE_CHUNK_BYTES is {BG_OVERWRITE_CHUNK_BYTES}; an overwrite chunk must be a whole number of tiles (32 B) and at least one, or BG_Stream_Update's `andi.w #$FFFC` length and the offset arithmetic stop landing on tile boundaries")
ensure(BG_OVERWRITE_CHUNK_BYTES <= DMA_BUDGET_NTSC - FG_PEAK_BYTES - BG_CRITICAL_PEAK_BYTES,
       "engine/level/bg.emp: an overwrite chunk of {BG_OVERWRITE_CHUNK_BYTES} B does not fit the NTSC DMA window ({DMA_BUDGET_NTSC}) after the foreground plane-drain peak ({FG_PEAK_BYTES}) and the Critical peak ({BG_CRITICAL_PEAK_BYTES}), even before any game rider. It could then never drain on a worst frame, and because the drain stops at the first entry that does not fit, it would hold every Deferrable entry queued behind it too")
ensure(BG_TILE_BASE_VRAM + BG_TILE_CAPACITY_BYTES <= $10000,
       "engine/level/bg.emp: the BG arena ends past $FFFF; BG_Stream_Update's overwrite spells the chunk destination and the arena bound as WORDS")

ensure(BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS >= 1,
       "engine/level/bg.emp: BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS is {BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS} — the streamer is allowed no rows at all, so BG_Plane_Top can never move and a map taller than the plane would show its first {PLANE_V_CELLS} rows forever while the scroll ran past them")

// -----------------------------------------------
// BG_Stream_Update — move the Plane B window to follow this frame's BG scroll.
//
// In:  nothing. Reads Region_Current (the crossing's cache), that row's
//      rg_bg_layout and rg_bg_span, Parallax_Current_Vscroll_BG (map space, already
//      clamped by Parallax_Step5_Vscroll THIS frame), BG_Plane_Layout and BG_Plane_Top.
// Out: nothing. Appends up to BG_WIPE_ROWS_PER_FRAME + BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS row entries to
//      Plane_Buffer. Advances BG_Plane_Top by one per ADMITTED steady-state entry, and
//      BG_Wipe_Row / BG_Wipe_Cursor by one per ADMITTED wipe entry.
//
// IT ALSO CARRIES THE CROSSING WIPE (step 6, §4.3), which is a second, independent job in
// one proc and is said here rather than left to be discovered. The wipe arm runs FIRST and
// ABOVE the span early-out; the reasoning for both, and for the level-triggered arm, is at
// the arm itself.
//
// CALL IT AFTER Parallax_Update AND BEFORE VBlank. Both halves matter: the scroll
// it reads is the one Step 5 just produced, and the row entries it appends drain
// in the SAME VBlank as the VSRAM word (VInt_DrawLevel and Vscroll_Write are both
// in the VBlank handler), so the new rows and the scroll that reveals them reach
// the hardware together. A tracker run before Parallax_Update would be one frame
// stale in a way the lead hides — which is worse than a tear, because it would
// only appear when the lead was already spent.
//
// EVERY SHIPPED RELEASE ROW LEAVES EARLY, at the second test: all of OJZ act 1's
// ten release rows take rg_bg_span = 0. The proc costs them 16 bytes of fetch and
// an rts, and moves no window, which is what "nothing visible changes before step
// 6" means on the release shape.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc BG_Stream_Update () clobbers(d0-d7/a0-a2) {
        // -- the region, READ and not re-resolved: Parallax_CheckBoundary wrote
        //    this cell earlier in the SAME frame, against the same camera. Null
        //    until the first crossing resolves, and null means the act default,
        //    which means the map is the plane, which means nothing to do. --
        movea.l Region_Current, a0
        cmpa.w  #0, a0
        beq     .done

        // ================= THE TILE OVERWRITE (region bg switch, 2026-09-16) =================
        // Plan: docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-16-region-bg-switch.md (tasks 3-4, calls C1-C8).
        // It runs FIRST, above the wipe and the streamer, and it GATES both: while the BG arena
        // does not hold this region's effective tile blob, this proc returns from `.ow_wait`
        // before either can paint a row. That return is the whole ordering guarantee. A row
        // painted from the new layout while its tiles are still arriving is S3K Icecap's
        // failure (a layout switch gated on the layout being ready, not on its tiles).
        //
        // -- the region's EFFECTIVE tile blob: its own, or the act default (rg_bg_layout's
        //    ladder and sentinel). Compared BY POINTER with what the arena holds. --
        movea.l Region.rg_bg_tiles(a0), a1
        cmpa.w  #0, a1
        bne     .ow_eff
        movea.l Current_Act_Ptr, a2                 // (Current_Act_Ptr).w
        movea.l Act.act_bg_tiles(a2), a1
    .ow_eff:
        cmpa.l  BG_Tiles_Current, a1                // (BG_Tiles_Current).w
        beq     .tiles_settled                      // the arena holds it: every frame, normally
        cmpa.l  BG_Tiles_Target, a1                 // (BG_Tiles_Target).w
        beq     .ow_spend                           // already uploading exactly this blob
        // ---- ARM, or RETARGET (C8: the target changed mid-overwrite — restart at 0) ----
        // Chunks already queued for an older target stay queued and land first (the queue is
        // FIFO and compacts in order); the completion test below waits for every arena write
        // to drain after THIS target's last chunk, so nothing stale can land after it.
        move.l  a1, BG_Tiles_Target
        clr.l   BG_Tiles_Current                    // the arena is now a PARTIAL blob
        clr.w   BG_Tiles_Offset
    .ow_spend:
        move.w  (a1), d3                            // blob length in bytes (BG_Init's header)
        cmpi.w  #BG_TILE_CAPACITY_BYTES, d3
        bls     .ow_len_ok
        move.w  #BG_TILE_CAPACITY_BYTES, d3         // runtime clamp (C12), as BG_UploadTiles
    .ow_len_ok:
        andi.w  #$FFFC, d3                          // longword-granular, as BG_UploadTiles
        // -- ONE OUTSTANDING CHUNK, AND COMPLETION, ARE THE SAME TEST (C2, as amended): no queued
        //    Deferrable entry may still be writing the arena. Not "the queue is empty": object DPLC
        //    art and waterline art share this queue, and an overwrite keyed to an empty queue
        //    would never finish while the player animates. --
        movem.l d3/a0-a1, -(sp)                     // movem leaves CCR alone on the way back
        move.w  #BG_TILE_BASE_VRAM, d1
        move.w  #BG_TILE_BASE_VRAM + BG_TILE_CAPACITY_BYTES, d2
        jbsr    DMA_Deferrable_DestPending          // Z SET = nothing queued writes the arena
        movem.l (sp)+, d3/a0-a1
        bne     .ow_wait                            // a chunk (or a pre-arm band DMA) is still queued
        move.w  BG_Tiles_Offset, d0                 // (BG_Tiles_Offset).w
        cmp.w   d3, d0
        bhs     .ow_complete                        // every chunk enqueued AND every one has landed
        sub.w   d0, d3                              // bytes left
        cmpi.w  #BG_OVERWRITE_CHUNK_BYTES, d3
        bls     .ow_chunk_len
        move.w  #BG_OVERWRITE_CHUNK_BYTES, d3
    .ow_chunk_len:
        moveq   #0, d1
        move.w  d0, d1
        addq.l  #2, d1
        add.l   a1, d1                              // src = blob + 2 + offset (ROM)
    if DEBUG == 1 {
        // The queue's `lsr.l #1` word-source conversion drops bit 0: an odd blob address would
        // DMA every chunk from one byte early (page_in.emp's raw-page precedent).
        btst    #0, d1
        beq     .ow_src_even
        raise_error "BG_Stream_Update: region tile blob at an odd address (DMA source)"
    .ow_src_even:
    }
        move.w  d0, d2
        addi.w  #BG_TILE_BASE_VRAM, d2              // dest = arena + offset
        movem.l d3/a0, -(sp)
        jbsr    QueueDMA_Deferrable                 // carry SET = queue full / byte cap: retry
        movem.l (sp)+, d3/a0
        bcs     .ow_wait
        add.w   d3, BG_Tiles_Offset                 // (BG_Tiles_Offset).w
    .ow_wait:
        // RETURNING HERE IS WHAT SUSPENDS THE WIPE AND THE STREAMER for the whole overwrite
        // (C7). Moving this `rts` below the wipe arm is the Icecap failure, and the gate's
        // ORDER legs exist to go red on exactly that edit.
        rts
    .ow_complete:
        move.l  (BG_Tiles_Target).w, (BG_Tiles_Current).w   // the arena holds the target, landed
        clr.l   BG_Tiles_Target
        // FALL THROUGH: the wipe arm below may fire on this same frame, which is correct —
        // the last chunk was SENT in an earlier VBlank (observed above), and this frame's wipe
        // rows drain in the next one.
    .tiles_settled:
        // ================= END OF THE TILE OVERWRITE =================

        // -- max_top = map rows - PLANE_V_CELLS, for THIS region. A 0 span is the act
        //    default, whose map IS the plane and whose only legal window is 0; a map no
        //    taller than the plane is the same case.
        //
        //    THIS USED TO BE TWO EARLY-OUTS (`beq .done` on a 0 span, `ble .done` on a map
        //    no taller than the plane) AND STEP 6 HAD TO DELETE THEM. Their reasoning —
        //    "a one-position window, so leave the window where it is" — is only true while
        //    BG_Plane_Top is ALREADY at that one position. Cross OUT of a taller-map region
        //    and it is not: the tracker is left at whatever top the tall map earned (up to
        //    32 on the step-5 fixture) with a new map that has no such row. Before step 6
        //    that was invisible — the streamer simply never moved again. With a wipe it is
        //    not invisible at all: the sweep derives its source map row from BG_Plane_Top,
        //    so a stale top makes it read PAST THE END of the new blob, one row at a time,
        //    for the whole sweep. Falling through to the clamp costs release ~6 bytes and
        //    leaves its behaviour identical (span 0 -> max_top 0 -> want_top 0 -> the
        //    `cmp/beq` below still finds the window already there on every shipped row).
        moveq   #0, d3                              // d3 = max_top, assumed 0
        move.w  Region.rg_bg_span(a0), d0           // (Region.rg_bg_span)(a0)
        beq     .max_ok                             // 0 = the act default: the map IS the plane
        lsr.w   #3, d0                              // span px -> map rows (BG_STREAM_ROW_PX)
        subi.w  #PLANE_V_CELLS, d0
        ble     .max_ok                             // map is not taller than the plane
        move.w  d0, d3                              // d3 = max_top
    .max_ok:

        // -- want_top = clamp(vrow - LEAD, 0, max_top). The scroll is non-negative
        //    on every path (step 4's position clamp floors it at 0), so lsr is the
        //    right shift and the only sign question is the subtraction below. --
        move.w  Parallax_Current_Vscroll_BG, d6     // (Parallax_Current_Vscroll_BG).w
        lsr.w   #3, d6                              // -> the top visible MAP row
        move.w  d6, d7                              // keep it: the wipe's start row wants it too
        subi.w  #BG_STREAM_LEAD_ROWS, d6
        bpl     .want_hi
        moveq   #0, d6                              // near the map top: no room for the lead
    .want_hi:
        cmp.w   d3, d6
        ble     .want_ok
        move.w  d3, d6                              // near the map bottom: ditto
    .want_ok:

        // ================= THE CROSSING WIPE (step 6, spec §4.3) =================
        // Everything from here to `.stream` is the wipe. It sits ABOVE the steady state and
        // BELOW the window arithmetic, and both halves of that placement are load-bearing:
        // it needs `want_top` (see the arm), and it must not be gated on the span, because
        // a region may name a DIFFERENT background of the SAME height (rg_bg_layout set,
        // rg_bg_span 0 — legal, and what ojz_region() allows). Gated on the span it would be
        // dead on exactly the second-layout showcase the spec's step 6 asks an author for.

        // -- the region's EFFECTIVE layout: its own, or the act default. Same ladder and
        //    same sentinel Draw_BG_TileRow and Section_RedrawPlanes use. --
        movea.l Region.rg_bg_layout(a0), a1
        cmpa.w  #0, a1
        bne     .eff_ok
        movea.l Current_Act_Ptr, a2                 // (Current_Act_Ptr).w
        movea.l Act.act_bg_layout(a2), a1
    .eff_ok:
        move.l  a1, d0
        cmp.l   BG_Plane_Layout, d0                 // (BG_Plane_Layout).w — what the plane HOLDS
        beq     .wipe_spend                         // unchanged: the common case, every frame

        // ---- ARM ----------------------------------------------------------------
        // LEVEL-TRIGGERED HERE, NOT EDGE-TRIGGERED AT THE CROSSING, and that is a
        // deliberate deviation from §4.3, which asks for a `BG_Region_Changed(a0)` call
        // inserted into Parallax_CheckBoundary between Effects_InstallPreset and
        // Parallax_StartTransition. THE REASON IS THE MODULE GRAPH: this file imports
        // engine.parallax (BG_VSCROLL_ROW_PX / BG_VSCROLL_MAX_STEP, so that the streamer's
        // budget is DERIVED from the rate clamp rather than restated), and the header of
        // BG_Stream_Update records "engine.parallax does not import this module, so the
        // edge is acyclic" as a designed property. A call the other way would close the
        // cycle, and the alternatives — hosting the arm in plane_buffer.emp, or having the
        // game loop detect the crossing itself — either move the background's identity out
        // of the background's file or create the second private answer to "which region am
        // I in" that step 3 refused.
        //
        // IT IS NOT A WEAKER TEST. `Region_Current` is written by the crossing and by
        // nothing else, this proc runs once a frame strictly AFTER Parallax_Update (the
        // call site is a `jbsr` pair in the game loop), and both synchronous Plane B
        // writers set BG_Plane_Layout to what they blitted. So the comparison above is
        // true on exactly the frames the crossing changed the answer, and it CANNOT
        // disagree with the crossing because it reads the crossing's own output. It is
        // also strictly better placed than the spec's site in one respect: the scroll it
        // latches below is THIS frame's, because Parallax_Update has already run, where
        // the §4.3 site would have read the previous frame's.
        //
        // WHAT IS NOT COMPARED, and why: §4.3 asks for "the layout pointer AND the tracked
        // span". There is no tracked span in RAM and none is added. A span changes the
        // WINDOW (max_top, below) and cannot change what any single map row contains, so a
        // span-only change is the steady-state tracker's business and re-sweeping 64 rows
        // for it would be 16 frames of redrawing the picture with itself.
        //
        // WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT DONE HERE: `st Parallax_Snap_Pending`, which §4.3 asks
        // for "so the scroll does not lerp across two different pictures". Measured at this
        // pin, it would not do that and would do something else. (1) The scroll it means is
        // Parallax_Step5_Vscroll's, and that proc's `tst.b Parallax_Snap_Pending` is DEAD —
        // Step 3's band loop clears the byte in the two instructions before its `jbra` into
        // Step 5 (booked as PARALLAX-STEP5-SNAP-DEAD, and re-read here rather than carried).
        // (2) What the flag DOES reach is Step 3's per-band `.snap_b`, which overrides the
        // frames-remaining ramp Parallax_StartTransition staged from the author's
        // `pcfg_transition` — a second authority for snap-versus-lerp at a crossing, which
        // is the one thing StartTransition exists to be. (3) The band drift accumulator is
        // added upstream of that test and is not reset by it, so the flag is not what
        // d-43 ("a drifting background CONTINUES across a boundary rather than resetting",
        // the owner's own words) forbids — but neither does anything here need it. Nothing
        // in this wipe touches a drift accumulator, a band scroll or the BG scroll: it
        // writes nametable rows and two tracker bytes.
        move.l  d0, BG_Plane_Layout                 // (BG_Plane_Layout).w — the plane is now PROMISED this blob
        // THE WINDOW SNAPS TO THE DESTINATION, and this is the second half of the fix the
        // deleted early-outs above describe. A wipe is an ASYNCHRONOUS PRIME: every plane
        // row is about to be rewritten, so the only window the tracker can usefully name is
        // the one the sweep is drawing TOWARDS. Section_RedrawPlanes does exactly this for
        // its synchronous prime — seed the top, then fill — and the symmetry is the point.
        //
        // It also makes the sweep's source rows in-bounds BY CONSTRUCTION: the map row for
        // plane row p is derived as T + ((p - T) mod 64), which lies in [T, T+63], and
        // want_top is clamped to [0, map_rows - 64], so every derived row is a row the new
        // blob has. Leave the old top here and the cross-OUT case reads past the blob.
        //
        // The tracker does momentarily describe a window the plane does not yet hold. That
        // is not a new lie — during a wipe the plane holds a MIX and no single window
        // describes it — and naming the destination is the only statement that stays true
        // at the end. Nothing but this proc reads BG_Plane_Top.
        move.w  d6, BG_Plane_Top                    // (BG_Plane_Top).w — want_top, computed above
        // THE START ROW: the top VISIBLE plane row. d7 is (vscroll >> 3), the top visible
        // MAP row, kept from the window arithmetic above; the plane is a ring, so its low
        // 6 bits ARE the plane row showing it — no window arithmetic at all.
        move.w  d7, d0
        andi.w  #PLANE_V_CELLS-1, d0                // -> the PLANE row showing it
        move.b  d0, BG_Wipe_Row                     // (BG_Wipe_Row).w
        move.b  #BG_WIPE_TOTAL_ROWS, BG_Wipe_Cursor // (BG_Wipe_Cursor).w

        // ---- SPEND ---------------------------------------------------------------
    .wipe_spend:
        tst.b   BG_Wipe_Cursor                      // (BG_Wipe_Cursor).w
        beq     .stream                             // no sweep in flight — the common case
        moveq   #BG_WIPE_ROWS_PER_FRAME, d4         // rows this frame may buy
    .wipe_loop:
        // d1 = the MAP row plane row p is CURRENTLY showing, derived from the LIVE window:
        //   m = T + ((p - T) mod PLANE_V_CELLS),  the unique m in [T, T+63] with m = p mod 64.
        // Deriving it instead of storing it is what makes the sweep survive a window that
        // moves mid-wipe — a FALL, which is the case §4.3 had no answer for. A stored map
        // row goes stale the moment BG_Plane_Top advances; a stored PLANE row does not,
        // because a plane row is a physical address and the map row it shows is a function
        // of the window at the instant of the draw.
        moveq   #0, d0
        move.b  BG_Wipe_Row, d0                     // (BG_Wipe_Row).w — the plane row, 0..63
        move.w  BG_Plane_Top, d1                    // (BG_Plane_Top).w — T
        move.w  d0, d3
        sub.w   d1, d3
        andi.w  #PLANE_V_CELLS-1, d3                // (p - T) mod the ring
        add.w   d3, d1                              // d1 = the map row
        moveq   #0, d2                              // band X: part 2's bands are WRAP bands
                                                    //   over a map exactly as wide as the
                                                    //   plane, anchor structurally 0 — the
                                                    //   same argument the streamer makes
        jbsr    Draw_BG_TileRow                     // d0 = 1 admitted, 0 refused; a0 survives
        tst.w   d0
        beq     .stream                             // REFUSED: spend nothing, retry next frame.
                                                    //   The cursor advances on 1 and only on 1
                                                    //   — that contract is why this producer
                                                    //   returns anything at all.
        moveq   #0, d0
        move.b  BG_Wipe_Row, d0
        addq.w  #1, d0
        andi.w  #PLANE_V_CELLS-1, d0                // walk DOWN the plane, wrapping
        move.b  d0, BG_Wipe_Row                     // (BG_Wipe_Row).w
        subq.b  #1, BG_Wipe_Cursor                  // (BG_Wipe_Cursor).w
        beq     .stream                             // the plane now holds the new picture
        subq.w  #1, d4
        bne     .wipe_loop
    .stream:
        // ================= END OF THE WIPE =================
        // d6 = want_top, computed above the wipe (the arm needs it too).

        // -- direction and budget. THE ORDER HERE IS LOAD-BEARING: moveq writes the
        //    condition codes, so both moveq must precede the cmp whose flags the
        //    beq and the blt read. Written the other way round the blt tests the
        //    moveq's result and the streamer walks the window the wrong way. --
        move.w  BG_Plane_Top, d4                    // (BG_Plane_Top).w — where it is
        moveq   #BG_STREAM_MAX_ROWS, d5             // rows left to spend this frame
        moveq   #1, d7                              // step: downward (top increasing)
        cmp.w   d6, d4
        beq     .done                               // already there — the common case
        blt     .loop
        moveq   #-1, d7                             // upward (top decreasing)
    .loop:
        // -- the map row ENTERING the window. Going down the window [T, T+63]
        //    becomes [T+1, T+64], so the new row is T + PLANE_V_CELLS; going up it
        //    becomes [T-1, T+62] and the new row is T - 1. --
        move.w  d4, d1
        tst.w   d7
        bmi     .ent_up
        addi.w  #PLANE_V_CELLS, d1
        jbra    .ent_ok
    .ent_up:
        subq.w  #1, d1
    .ent_ok:
        move.w  d1, d0
        andi.w  #PLANE_V_CELLS-1, d0                // plane row = map row mod the ring
        moveq   #0, d2                              // the band's layout X: part 2's bands
                                                    //   are all WRAP over a map exactly as
                                                    //   wide as the plane, where the anchor
                                                    //   is structurally 0 (Draw_BG_TileRow)
        jbsr    Draw_BG_TileRow                     // d0 = 1 admitted, 0 refused
        tst.w   d0
        beq     .done                               // REFUSED: do not advance the window.
                                                    //   The cursor advances on 1 and only on
                                                    //   1 — that contract is the whole reason
                                                    //   this producer returns anything.
        add.w   d7, d4
        move.w  d4, BG_Plane_Top                    // (BG_Plane_Top).w
        cmp.w   d6, d4
        beq     .done
        subq.w  #1, d5
        bne     .loop
    .done:
        rts
}

// §4.2: BG_RedrawForSection deleted. Plane B is redrawn atomically alongside
// Plane A via Section_RedrawPlanes (see engine/level/section.emp), triggered
// by the Section_Plane_Dirty flag at level init and cache recovery only.
// Teleports no longer set it: they are pure coordinate rebases — world
// coordinates, plane mapping (mod 64) and scroll (mod 512) are all invariant
// under the $1000px shift, so a redraw would write byte-identical content
// (docs/research/teleport-rebase.md).

// ---- THE PLANE GEOMETRY FAMILY, pinned across ALL its members in this file (2026-09-11) ----
// The LS-8a plane-wrap pins at the end of engine/level/section.emp and plane_buffer.emp named
// this file as hand-spelling the same PLANE_H_CELLS x PLANE_V_CELLS geometry, unpinned. The
// sites were enumerated by what TOUCHES the plane size (every constant and instruction whose
// value must move if the plane does, read over the whole file), not by grepping a literal.
//
// THE TWO PER-AXIS PINS WERE DELETED ON 2026-09-15 (regions part 2, step 2) BECAUSE THEIR
// SITES WERE. They named three instructions, all of them in BG_Init's nametable blit and all
// of them artefacts of the column-major order: `move.w #$8F80` and `cmpi.w #64` (horizontal)
// and `moveq #32-1` (vertical). The row-major blit is one linear `move.l` run, so its three
// remaining literals are the autoincrement $02 (a CELL, not a plane), the plane's VRAM base
// (a layout constant, not a size) and `BG_LAYOUT_SIZE/4 - 1`, which FOLLOWS the constant and
// so needs no pin at all. Deleting them is not a loosening: an `ensure` whose enumerated
// sites no longer exist cannot fail on the change it names, and leaving it would have read as
// coverage this file no longer needs (docs/DEFERRED_WORK's vacuous-gate rule).
//
// BG_LAYOUT_SIZE keeps its OWN pin, and it is the only one left here: it depends on both axes
// and is still hand-spelled. It was already separate for this reason — folded into the axis
// pins, a vertical-only move fired the HORIZONTAL message too and blamed an axis that had not
// moved (measured 2026-09-11, M25/M26).
ensure(BG_LAYOUT_SIZE == PLANE_H_CELLS * PLANE_V_CELLS * 2,
       "BG LAYOUT SIZE (engine/level/bg.emp, 2026-09-11). engine/level/bg.emp's BG_LAYOUT_SIZE no longer matches the plane: it is {BG_LAYOUT_SIZE} bytes, and a full Plane B nametable is PLANE_H_CELLS x PLANE_V_CELLS x 2 = {PLANE_H_CELLS} x {PLANE_V_CELLS} x 2 = {PLANE_H_CELLS * PLANE_V_CELLS * 2}. THE SITE: `pub const BG_LAYOUT_SIZE = 64*64*2`, which BG_Init's nametable blit drains whole as one linear run, which Section_RedrawPlanes' plane-B blit drains the same way, which Draw_BG_TileRow (engine/level/plane_buffer.emp) reads one ROW of, and which games/sonic4/data/levels/ojz/act1/act_assets.emp uses as the embed type of OJZ_Act1_BG_Layout. IT IS NOW THE ONLY PIN IN THIS FILE: the two per-axis pins that stood beside it were deleted in regions part 2 step 2 with the three column-major literals they enumerated (see the family header above), so nothing fires beside this one any more and a plane-size move reaches this file through this message alone. WHAT THIS PIN DOES NOT COVER: the layout's ROW-MAJOR order, which no size can express — and which is exactly what a wrong-order blob would get wrong while passing this check; the count BG_Init and Section_RedrawPlanes spell from it (BG_LAYOUT_SIZE/4 - 1 and PLANE_H_CELLS * PLANE_V_CELLS / 2 - 1), which follow their constants; and the out-of-assembler writers of the blob (tools/inject_editor_bg.py, tools/ojz_strip_gen.py), which this pin cannot see")

// ---- THE BG PLANE TRACKER's one-word clear, pinned (regions part 2 step 3) ----
// Both synchronous writers of Plane B — BG_Init above and Section_RedrawPlanes
// (engine/level/section.emp) — retire the wipe state with a single `clr.w
// BG_Wipe_Cursor`, which is only "cursor AND resume row" while the two bytes are
// adjacent in that order. A field inserted between them would not fail to build; it
// would leave BG_Wipe_Row holding whatever the previous act left there while the
// cursor read 0. That is now WORSE than it was when this pin was written for
// BG_Wipe_Dir, because the arm below writes the row byte and the cursor byte as two
// separate instructions: a stale row byte survives only until the next arm, but a
// `clr.w` that has stopped covering the row byte means a PRIME leaves a cursor of 0
// beside a row the next sweep never chose. Pinned rather than spelled as two `clr.b`
// because the one-word form is what the two sites actually emit.
ensure(extern("BG_Wipe_Row") - extern("BG_Wipe_Cursor") == 1,
       "engine/ram.emp's BG_Wipe_Cursor and BG_Wipe_Row are no longer adjacent bytes in that order (BG_Wipe_Row - BG_Wipe_Cursor is not 1). BOTH synchronous Plane B writers clear them with ONE `clr.w BG_Wipe_Cursor` — engine/level/bg.emp's BG_Init and engine/level/section.emp's Section_RedrawPlanes — so the pair must stay a word. Either restore the adjacency or respell both sites as two `clr.b`")
